{"qid":"Q178913","names":["Empire of Trebizond"],"title":"Empire of Trebizond","endonym":{"native":"Αυτοκρατορία της Τραπεζούντας","lang":"el"},"capsule":"The Empire of Trebizond was founded in April 1204 when Alexios I Komnenos, aided by Georgian forces under Queen Tamar, seized the Black Sea port of Trebizond (modern Trabzon) days before the Fourth Crusade sacked Constantinople; Alexios proclaimed himself emperor of the Romans. The empire survived as a Mongol vassal from 1243, formally renouncing claims to Constantinople under John II in 1282 and thereafter styling its rulers 'Emperors of the East, Iberia, and Perateia'; its Pontic court maintained Byzantine literary traditions and profited from Black Sea trade routes linking Central Asia to Genoese and Venetian merchants. Mehmed II isolated Trebizond diplomatically before besieging it; Emperor David surrendered on 15 August 1461, ending the last surviving fragment of Byzantine imperial rule.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The empire's Pontic coastal core is well-attested; inland borders in the Pontic highlands and Black Sea outposts fluctuated considerably with Mongol and Turkmen pressure, introducing moderate uncertainty into mapped extents especially for the thirteenth century.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Empire of Trebizond","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empire_of_Trebizond"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Alexios I of Trebizond","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_I_of_Trebizond"},{"label":"Wikipedia: David of Trebizond","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_of_Trebizond"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikiped